ubernetes Ingress

Today I learned about Ingress in Kubernetes and how it is used to route external HTTP/HTTPS traffic to multiple Kubernetes Services using routing rules.

What I learned
Ingress provides a single entry point for external HTTP/HTTPS traffic.
It routes requests to Services, which then forward traffic to Pods.
We can route traffic using paths like /, /api, /admin or different hosts.
Ingress Controller actually handles the incoming traffic.
HTTPS can be configured using a TLS Secret.
Traffic Flow
User
 ↓
LoadBalancer
 ↓
Ingress Controller
 ↓
Ingress Rules
 ↓
Service
 ↓
Pod
Example Ingress YAML
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress

metadata:
  name: myapp-ingress

spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx

  rules:
    - host: myapp.com
      http:
        paths:

          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: frontend-service
                port:
                  number: 80

          - path: /api
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: backend-service
                port:
                  number: 80

          - path: /admin
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: admin-service
                port:
                  number: 80
Routing
myapp.com/        → frontend-service
myapp.com/api     → backend-service
myapp.com/admin   → admin-service
HTTPS / TLS
tls:
  - hosts:
      - myapp.com
    secretName: myapp-tls

Key takeaway:

Ingress → routes external HTTP/HTTPS traffic → to multiple Services → which forward traffic to Pods.